          "question": "is it worth it to keep working",
          "answer": "I\u2019m going by your record, which places you in Article 11, with 21.5 years of service at age 56 \u2014 tell me if that\u2019s not right.\n\nYou appear eligible to retire now under Section 11-130. It applies to an employee age 55 or older but younger than 60 who has at least 10 years of service. With 20 or more years of service, it provides an age-and-service annuity, plus prior-service annuity where applicable, based on the amounts credited at withdrawal and your age then. [40 ILCS 5/11-130]\n\nContinuing to work can increase your pension because additional service and contributions may increase the amounts credited to you. The statute text in front of me does not provide enough information to say how much your pension would increase. It would depend on your salary, contribution history, prior-service credit, and the fund\u2019s calculation.\n\nAt age 60, Section 11-129 would apply. It provides an annuity based on the total amounts accumulated for the applicable annuities, computed at your age when you withdraw. [40 ILCS 5/11-129] The text I have does not establish that waiting until age 60 produces a particular percentage increase or a specific reduction at age 56.\n\nYour employee contribution for the age-and-service annuity is generally 6.5% of salary under Section 11-170, although that section contains exceptions tied to provisions I have not read. [40 ILCS 5/11-170]\n\nI could not establish a pension amount, an exact age-56-versus-age-60 comparison, or whether any other election or adjustment changes your result. The provisions that could affect those questions include sections I have not read, including Section 11-134 and related provisions.\n\nSo, under the sections available here: you can retire now, but the Code does not provide enough information to decide whether stopping now or continuing to work is financially better for you. The key comparison is the fund\u2019s projected annuity at your planned retirement dates, using your actual salary and service records.",
